.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[CR]osslsigncode\f[R] signs and verifies Microsoft Authenticode
signatures on supported file formats.
It can also extract data for detached signing, attach an externally
produced signature, add timestamps or unauthenticated blobs to an
existing signature, and remove an embedded signature.
.PP
Supported input formats include PE files such as EXE, DLL, and SYS, CAB,
CAT, MSI, APPX, and several script file types, including
\f[CR].ps1\f[R], \f[CR].ps1xml\f[R], \f[CR].psc1\f[R], \f[CR].psd1\f[R],
\f[CR].psm1\f[R], \f[CR].cdxml\f[R], \f[CR].mof\f[R], and
\f[CR].js\f[R].
.PP
The program supports these common workflows:
.IP \(bu 2
direct signing of an unsigned file
.IP \(bu 2
detached signing via \f[CR]extract\-data\f[R], \f[CR]sign\f[R], and
\f[CR]attach\-signature\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
post\-sign timestamping with \f[CR]add\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
verification of embedded signatures or catalog signatures with
\f[CR]verify\f[R]
.PP
If no subcommand is given, \f[CR]sign\f[R] is assumed.
.SH FORMATS
Support is not identical across all file formats.
.PP
In particular, detached\-signature workflows, nested signatures,
catalog\-based verification, and signature removal are format\-dependent
features.
A command that is valid for one supported file type may be unsupported
for another.
.PP
CAT files are a special case.
They are detached catalog containers for hashes of other files, not
ordinary embedded\-signature payloads.
A CAT file is itself a PKCS#7 structure containing authenticated entries
for one or more external files.
In practice, the catalog signs file digests recorded in the catalog,
rather than embedding a signature into each covered file.
.PP
Because of this, CAT files behave differently from embedded\-signature
formats.
They do not support \f[CR]attach\-signature\f[R],
\f[CR]remove\-signature\f[R], \f[CR]extract\-data\f[R], or nested
signatures.
.PP
MSI files are also a special case.
They support an extended signature mode controlled by
\f[CR]\-add\-msi\-dse\f[R].
In this mode, the MSI signature covers file metadata as well as file
content.
Detached\-signing workflows and any later re\-signing or nesting
operations must use a mode consistent with the MSI file\(cqs existing
signature structure.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS \f[CR]sign\f[R]
Create a new Authenticode signature.
.PP
This command can sign a normal unsigned file, or it can sign PKCS#7 data
previously produced by \f[CR]extract\-data\f[R].
.SS \f[CR]extract\-data\f[R]
Extract the PKCS#7 content to be signed later.
This is used for detached signing workflows.
.SS \f[CR]add\f[R]
Add unauthenticated attributes to an existing signature, typically an
Authenticode timestamp, an RFC 3161 timestamp, or an unauthenticated
blob.
.PP
With \f[CR]\-index\f[R], the selected signature in a multi\-signature
file is updated.
.SS \f[CR]attach\-signature\f[R]
Attach a detached PKCS#7 signature to an input file.
.PP
With \f[CR]\-nest\f[R], the new signature is attached as a nested
signature instead of replacing the primary one, if the file format
supports nested signatures.
.SS \f[CR]extract\-signature\f[R]
Extract the embedded PKCS#7 signature from a signed file.
.SS \f[CR]remove\-signature\f[R]
Remove the embedded signature from a signed file.
.SS \f[CR]verify\f[R]
Verify an embedded signature or a catalog signature.
.PP
Verification may include digest consistency, certificate chain
validation, certificate revocation checking, timestamp validation, and
optional checking of the signer\(cqs leaf certificate hash.
.PP
When verifying that a file is covered by a catalog, use
\f[CR]verify \-catalog catalog.cat \-in file\f[R].
Verifying the CAT file by itself validates the catalog signature;
verifying with \f[CR]\-catalog\f[R] checks whether the specified input
file is covered by that catalog.
