.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Common causes of failure include:
.TP
missing CA trust bundle
On Unix\-like systems, \f[CR]verify\f[R] expects a readable CA bundle,
either from \f[CR]\-CAfile\f[R] or from a detected system default.
.TP
detached\-signing mismatch
\f[CR]extract\-data\f[R], \f[CR]sign\f[R], and
\f[CR]attach\-signature\f[R] must use compatible digest\-affecting
options such as \f[CR]\-h\f[R], and where relevant \f[CR]\-ph\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-add\-msi\-dse\f[R].
.TP
unsupported format feature
Some file formats do not support every subcommand or every signature
mode.
.TP
missing TSA trust chain
Timestamp verification may fail unless the appropriate TSA trust anchors
are supplied with \f[CR]\-TSA\-CAfile\f[R], and where needed
\f[CR]\-TSA\-CRLfile\f[R].
.TP
conflicting timestamp modes
\f[CR]\-t\f[R], \f[CR]\-ts\f[R], and built\-in TSA signing cannot be
combined in one command.
.TP
MSI signature mode mismatch
Re\-signing or nesting an MSI signature must be consistent with whether
the file already uses \f[CR]MsiDigitalSignatureEx\f[R].
Mixing modes may invalidate the existing signature.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\f[CR]HTTP_PROXY\f[R], \f[CR]http_proxy\f[R]
Default proxy for HTTP access if \f[CR]\-p\f[R] is not given.
.TP
\f[CR]HTTPS_PROXY\f[R], \f[CR]https_proxy\f[R]
Default proxy for HTTPS access if \f[CR]\-p\f[R] is not given.
.TP
\f[CR]OPENSSL_ENGINES\f[R]
May help OpenSSL find engine modules.
.SH FILES
On Unix\-like systems, \f[CR]osslsigncode\f[R] tries common CA bundle
locations for its default \f[CR]\-CAfile\f[R], including:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]/etc/ssl/certs/ca\-certificates.crt\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]/etc/pki/tls/certs/ca\-bundle.crt\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]/usr/share/ssl/certs/ca\-bundle.crt\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]/usr/local/share/certs/ca\-root\-nss.crt\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]/etc/ssl/cert.pem\f[R]
.PP
If no readable CA bundle is available, \f[CR]verify\f[R] may require an
explicit \f[CR]\-CAfile\f[R].
.SH NOTES
Use \f[CR]extract\-data\f[R] when you need to create a new detached
signature object.
Use \f[CR]extract\-signature\f[R] when you need to copy an existing
embedded PKCS#7 signature out of a file.
.PP
For safer secret handling, prefer \f[CR]\-readpass\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-askpass\f[R] over \f[CR]\-pass\f[R].
.PP
Data added with \f[CR]\-addUnauthenticatedBlob\f[R] is not protected by
the signature and must not be treated as trusted.
.PP
For new MSI signatures, \f[CR]\-add\-msi\-dse\f[R] is generally
preferred because it extends signing coverage to MSI metadata as well as
file content.
However, it is format\-affecting rather than cosmetic, so existing
signed MSI files should be re\-signed only in a mode consistent with
their current signature structure.
.PP
Output files are not overwritten.
